<releaseinfo>Fontmatrix 0.11</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>&fontmatrix; is a font manager for adventurous graphic designers
and typesetters who need to manage hundreds and even thousands of
fonts.</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>KDE</keyword>
<keyword>fontmatrix</keyword>
<keyword>font</keyword>
<keyword>typography</keyword>
<keyword>OpenType</keyword>
<keyword>PANOSE</keyword>
</keywordset>

</bookinfo>


<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>&fontmatrix; is an end-user font manager originally designed for
Linux, with builds also available for Windows and macOS.</para>

<para>Its highlights are:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>works with local and remote font storages;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>features activation and deactivation of fonts based on
tags;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>allows finding suitable fonts by constructing simple or
complex queries;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>allows refining a fonts selection by comparing either
glyphs in detail or text lines;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>supports searching in PANOSE metadata and allows
non-destructively editing it;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>has advanced OpenType support;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>ships with sample texts in many languages and scripts to
assist testing fonts with wide Unicode coverage;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>features easy template-based creation of font books
(PDF).</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

</chapter>


<chapter id="basics">
<title>The Very Basics</title>

<para>This chapter explains the very basic things about font managers,
&fontmatrix; and fonts. If you don't know whether &fontmatrix; is of any
use to you, or you need to clear up one or two things about fonts, you
are exactly the person we wrote it for.</para>

<sect1 id="why-management">
<title>Why Use a Font Manager</title>

<para>This question pops up every now and then. Really, why? Aren't
people just happy with what they've got? Isn't installing and removing
fonts the usual way good enough? There are several reasons.</para>

<para>Designers working under contract are doomed to use a variety of
fonts, because every client has his or her own preferences and ideas
about what suits a particular task.</para>

<para>So we are talking here about collections of fonts that are as
large as 3,000 or 5,000 fonts: decorative freeware fonts, licensed
fonts, very expensive licensed font families with over 20 faces, and so
on. Keeping all of them loaded in the system is likely to slow it down
and make browsing through font lists very tedious. Which means you need
some tool to activate and deactivate fonts selectively.</para>

<para>What else? Searching. If you have over a thousand fonts you need
to be able to find the one you need quickly given that usually you have
only a basic idea of what you need. So you want to compare them: type
in some text and see how all candidates render this text, then pick the
one that suits the job best.</para>

<para>How about grouping some fonts? Think of branding projects: you
create a whole package — website, brochures, business cards — and use a
fixed set of fonts. At any time you have to be able to quickly enable
these fonts in the system to create a new brochure or a new bunch of
business cards for this client. Font managers help you here through a
tagging system.</para>

<para>If you work for a font foundry, your job is to promote fonts.
The most convenient way is to create a so called <emphasis>font
book</emphasis> — an album displaying how the same text is rendered
with several fonts. You filter the whole collection to select just the
fonts you want and in a few clicks you have a PDF file ready to be
uploaded or printed.</para>

<para>And the last bit. If you ever used complex design software, you
know that sometimes it finds a corrupt font and refuses to work. Font
managers often have built-in font validation tools that help to find
problematic fonts and deactivate them temporarily.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="why-fontmatrix">
<title>Why &fontmatrix;</title>

<para>So, what's so good about &fontmatrix;?</para>

<para><emphasis>It works wherever you go to.</emphasis> It doesn't
matter if you are a Linux, Windows or macOS user. You can keep all of
them around and still be using the same app everywhere.</para>

<para><emphasis>You don't need to pay for every installed
copy.</emphasis> &fontmatrix; is free software. We made it because we
needed it for our work and we share it with you.</para>

<para><emphasis>On Linux, this is one of the actively maintained font
managers.</emphasis> There are simpler tools, but few are still
maintained.</para>

<para><emphasis>You can actually affect the development of
&fontmatrix;.</emphasis> We appreciate interesting ideas and bug
reports. If you can fix a bug or implement a new function, see <xref
linkend="contributing"/>.</para>

<para><emphasis>You can use &fontmatrix; in your native
language.</emphasis> Both the user interface and documentation are
localizable.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="interface">
<title>User Interface</title>

<para>&fontmatrix; has both traditionally and not so traditionally
implemented functionality. The most important user interface parts you
will see are:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Menu bar, on top of the window</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Main area, with tabs representing one or another
mode</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Browse Fonts</guilabel>
sidebar</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Tags</guilabel> sidebar</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Status bar</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>System tray icon</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<sect1 id="ui-menubar">
<title>Menu bar</title>

<para>Menu items are logically divided into top-level menus:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guimenu>File</guimenu> — input and output
operations.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guimenu>Edit</guimenu> — operates on items currently
selected or filtered in the <guilabel>Browse Fonts</guilabel>
sidebar.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guimenu>View</guimenu> — controls visibility of
panels.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guimenu>Service</guimenu> — additional tools.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guimenu>Settings</guimenu> — configuration and toolbar
visibility.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guimenu>Help</guimenu> — opens this handbook and the
About dialog.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="ui-mainarea">
<title>Main area</title>

<para>The main area uses a tabbed interface. The first three tabs
(<guilabel>Font Information</guilabel>, <guilabel>Sample Text</guilabel>
and <guilabel>Glyphs</guilabel>) are related to viewing fonts. The
<guilabel>Playground</guilabel>, <guilabel>Classification</guilabel>
and <guilabel>Compare</guilabel> tabs are related to refining font
selection by comparison.</para>

<para>The main area and the <guilabel>Browse Fonts</guilabel> sidebar
have mostly a one-way connection: changes in the sidebar update the
main area, not the other way round, except for the
<guilabel>Classification</guilabel> tab which has a two-way
connection.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="ui-browse-sidebar">
<title>Browse Fonts sidebar</title>

<para>This sidebar is the gateway to your font collection. Apart from
letting you search using metadata queries, it lists fonts already added
to the database and fonts available on the local system and mounted
remote systems. Functionality is divided into three tabs.</para>

<sect2 id="ui-browse-names">
<title>Names</title>
<para>Fonts already added to the database are listed here, sorted
alphabetically. They are grouped on three levels: initial letter, name
of the typeface, and the variant (style). Activation and deactivation
of fonts is described in <xref linkend="management-activation"/>.</para>

<para><guibutton>Collapse or expand</guibutton> opens a drop-down menu
with items controlling the level of detail. <guibutton>Show face
boxes</guibutton> toggles checkboxes in front of font family faces,
allowing you to selectively activate or deactivate them.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="ui-browse-previews">
<title>Previews</title>
<para>In this tab &fontmatrix; renders short text samples with every
filtered font, sorted alphabetically. The text and font size of
previews can be changed below the previews. By default previews render
the names of the fonts themselves, using special placeholders:
<literal>&lt;name&gt;</literal>, <literal>&lt;family&gt;</literal> and
<literal>&lt;variant&gt;</literal>.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="ui-browse-folders">
<title>Folders</title>
<para>You can preview typefaces of installed fonts in the system Fonts
view, browse the Files view, or select any folder to preview all
typefaces located there.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="ui-tags-sidebar">
<title>Tags sidebar</title>
<para>This is where you manage tags and assign them to currently
selected fonts. See <xref linkend="management-tags"/> for details.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="ui-statusbar">
<title>Status bar</title>
<para>The left part displays hints for menu items. The right side names
the currently selected font and lists the count of currently filtered
fonts.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="ui-systray">
<title>System tray</title>
<para>The system tray icon allows controlling &fontmatrix; without
keeping the main window visible. Right-click toggles main-window
visibility; left-click reveals a menu. This feature can be enabled or
disabled in the Preferences dialog.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="management">
<title>Managing the Font Collection</title>

<para>This chapter explains how to use &fontmatrix; for effective
management of your fonts collection.</para>

<sect1 id="management-importing">
<title>Importing fonts</title>
<para>For this purpose three means are at your disposal:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Select <menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Import
Directory&hellip;</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. You will be asked to choose
a directory which is then explored recursively to import all font
files inside.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Drag-and-drop a selection of files into
&fontmatrix;.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Use the context menu for files and folders in the
<guilabel>Folders</guilabel> tab inside the <guilabel>Browse
Fonts</guilabel> sidebar.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="management-activation">
<title>Activating and deactivating fonts</title>
<para>There are three ways to activate and deactivate fonts in
&fontmatrix;:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Selective.</emphasis> In <guilabel>Browse
Fonts</guilabel>, go to the <guilabel>Names</guilabel> tab and enable
or disable particular font faces or whole families.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Mass via menu.</emphasis> Filter fonts (for
example by selecting a tag) and use
<menuchoice><guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Activate all
current</guimenuitem></menuchoice> or
<menuchoice><guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Deactivate all
current</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis>Mass via system tray.</emphasis> Right-click
the system tray icon and click on a tag name to activate or deactivate
fonts that have this tag.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="management-tags">
<title>Using tags</title>

<sect2 id="management-tags-why"><title>Why</title>
<para>Tags are a flexible way to categorize fonts because they don't
set any restrictions on how you organize them. Examples:
&quot;Slab serif fonts my wife hates&quot;, &quot;Fonts I designed for
COMPANY_NAME&quot;, &quot;Serif high-contrast text fonts I'm allowed to
embed&quot;.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="management-tags-add"><title>How to add tags</title>
<para>Two ways:</para>
<para>The first one is when you import a directory of fonts (and
<guilabel>Ask for tag names when importing fonts</guilabel> is enabled
in Preferences). A dialog lists existing tags and lets you pick or add
a new one.</para>
<para>If you want to assign tags to currently filtered fonts, use
<menuchoice><guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Tag all
filtered&hellip;</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="management-tags-edit"><title>Editing and removing tags</title>
<para>Right-click on a tag in the <guilabel>Tags</guilabel> panel to
edit or remove it from the database.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="viewing">
<title>Viewing Fonts</title>

<para>This chapter covers viewing various aspects of fonts, from
summary to Unicode block coverage.</para>

<sect1 id="viewing-overview">
<title>Font overview</title>
<para>Basic information about a font is summarized in the
<guilabel>Font Information</guilabel> tab, open by default. It
lists:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>full name of the font and its location;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>glyph count and font file type;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>information about designer and vendor;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>license URL and full text where available;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>PANOSE metadata, where available;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>terms of use (embedding, editing,
&etc;).</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>The Information area is rendered with QtWebEngine, so its
appearance is customizable via CSS — see <xref
linkend="prefs-display"/>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="viewing-sample-text">
<title>Sample text</title>
<para>The <guilabel>Sample Text</guilabel> tab can render any sample
on a large pannable canvas.</para>

<sect2 id="viewing-sample-collection">
<title>Sample text collection</title>
<para>&fontmatrix; ships with a set of sample texts in various
languages and scripts and lets you add your own (see <xref
linkend="prefs-samples"/>). To pick a text from the collection click
the <guibutton>Samples collection</guibutton> button to reveal a
sidebar with the available samples.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="viewing-sample-navigation">
<title>Navigation and rendering</title>
<para>Use <keycombo>&Ctrl;<mousebutton>wheel</mousebutton></keycombo>
to zoom and the middle mouse button to pan. Click and drag to draw a
zoom rectangle. A single click anywhere on the canvas resets to 1:1
zoom.</para>
<para>By default &fontmatrix; uses FreeType to render text — switch to
<emphasis>Vector</emphasis> in the <guilabel>Display
settings</guilabel> sidebar for smooth rescaling.</para>
<para>The <guilabel>World is wide</guilabel> options control inline
and block progression for non-Latin scripts (Hebrew, Mongolian,
&etc;).</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="viewing-sample-otf">
<title>OpenType features</title>
<para>Click the <guibutton>OpenType features</guibutton> button to
reveal a sidebar listing every available OpenType feature in the
loaded font, grouped by table and subtable. Toggling a feature
re-renders the canvas immediately.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="viewing-glyphs-chart">
<title>Glyphs chart</title>
<para>In <guilabel>Glyphs</guilabel> mode &fontmatrix; displays a
chart of glyphs present in the currently selected font, grouped into
Unicode blocks with coverage percentages.</para>
<para>Clicking on a glyph displays a larger version with metrics and
category. Switch to <guilabel>View all mapped glyphs</guilabel> and
type in the search field to find a glyph by name.</para>
<para>The <guibutton>Copy Mode</guibutton> button activates an entry
field used as a digital letter case: enable it, click on glyphs to
copy them in, then select all and copy to the clipboard.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="searching">
<title>Searching for Fonts</title>

<para>&fontmatrix; provides several ways to refine your selection
while looking for the best fit for a job. Use the generic metadata or
PANOSE search first to go from thousands to tens, then use a
<link linkend="comparisons">comparison</link> function to pick the
final font.</para>

<sect1 id="search-metadata">
<title>Generic metadata fields search</title>
<para>The upper part of the <guilabel>Browse Fonts</guilabel> sidebar
lets you build queries to filter fonts by metadata or tags.</para>
<para>By default &fontmatrix; searches all fields. Click the field
button to narrow the search to a specific field
(<guilabel>Copyright</guilabel>, <guilabel>Designer</guilabel>, &etc;),
type your text and press <keycap>Enter</keycap>.</para>
<para>For complex queries, toggle the <guilabel>And</guilabel> and
<guilabel>Not</guilabel> switches. The combined query is shown at the
bottom — for example <literal>[deja][!italic]</literal> matches all
non-italic DejaVu fonts.</para>
<para>Click <guibutton>reset</guibutton> to clear the query.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="search-panose">
<title>PANOSE metadata search</title>
<para>In the <guilabel>Classification</guilabel> tab a chart shows a
PANOSE map of the available fonts. Each axis represents a PANOSE
variable. Hover over an axis, click on a value, and the sidebar
filters to fonts matching that value. Hold <keycap>Shift</keycap> and
click another value on the same axis to extend the search.</para>
<para>If the PANOSE information is missing or incorrect, you can edit
it non-destructively — see <xref
linkend="additional-edit-panose"/>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="search-bitmap">
<title>Searching using bitmap samples</title>
<para>If you have an enormous fonts collection, you can search for a
font using a bitmap sample of one of its glyphs. Either feed
&fontmatrix; a ready bitmap file or use <emphasis>Grab</emphasis> mode
to fetch an image from your display.</para>
<para>Click on the glyph in the raster sample preview so &fontmatrix;
can compute its bounding box. If the box is wrong, enable
<guilabel>Tweak rect</guilabel> and drag the corners. Type the
character that the sample shows and press <guibutton>Search</guibutton>.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="comparisons">
<title>Comparisons</title>

<para>&fontmatrix; provides two ways to compare fonts: by comparing
lines of text in the <guilabel>Playground</guilabel> tab and by
comparing standalone glyphs in the <guilabel>Compare</guilabel>
tab.</para>

<sect1 id="comparisons-fonts">
<title>Comparison of fonts</title>

<sect2 id="comparisons-fonts-add">
<title>Adding fonts for comparison</title>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>In <guilabel>Browse Fonts</guilabel>, click on the
font you want to add.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Pick a font size of interest.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Click anywhere on the canvas — a text cursor flashes
where your text will start.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Type some text.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Click on another font in the sidebar to add another
line.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Repeat steps 1–5 until done.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="comparisons-fonts-compare">
<title>Comparing fonts</title>
<para>The <guilabel>Playground</guilabel> uses a huge zoomable
canvas where you can drag text lines around, regroup them and pan.
Unlike <guilabel>Sample Text</guilabel>, the playground always uses
Qt's native font rendering engine, so you can zoom freely without
seeing pixel blocks.</para>
<para>Hover over a text line to see a tooltip with the font name and
file location. Drag a line to move it; <keycombo>&Ctrl;
<mousebutton>click</mousebutton></keycombo> or rubber-band to select
several lines and move them together. Press <keycap>Esc</keycap> to
clear the selection.</para>
<para>Press the middle mouse button to pan;
<keycombo>&Ctrl;<mousebutton>wheel</mousebutton></keycombo> to
zoom.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="comparisons-fonts-clean">
<title>Cleaning the playground</title>
<para>To remove existing text lines, select them and press
<keycap>Delete</keycap>.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="comparisons-glyphs">
<title>Comparison of glyphs</title>
<para>Comparing the same glyph rendered by different fonts can be
useful for both work and study. To add a font to the list for
comparison, click its face in <guilabel>Browse Fonts</guilabel>, then
go to the <guilabel>Compare</guilabel> tab and click
<guibutton>Add</guibutton>.</para>

<para>Available options:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Show</guilabel> toggles visibility of the
glyph rendering for the currently selected font.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Fill</guilabel> chooses one of the predefined
fills, or <emphasis>None</emphasis>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Points</guilabel> renders the glyph's outline
points; <guilabel>Controls</guilabel> renders the control
points.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Metrics</guilabel> renders baseline and em
size.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Offset</guilabel> moves a glyph horizontally
so several glyphs can be lined up without
stacking.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Keep in sync</guilabel> ensures all fonts
show the same character. Disable it to compare different glyphs of the
same font.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="output">
<title>Exporting and Printing</title>

<para>This chapter covers exporting fonts, printing fonts, and
creating font books in PDF using templates.</para>

<sect1 id="export-fonts">
<title>Exporting fonts</title>
<para>Export of fonts in &fontmatrix; is tag-based: you can only export
fonts that have a particular tag.</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>Choose <menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu>
<guimenuitem>Export fonts&hellip;</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Pick a tag.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Pick a destination directory.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
<para>A progress indicator runs; when it vanishes you can find the
exported font files plus generated thumbnails in the destination.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="printing">
<title>Printing fonts</title>
<para>&fontmatrix; will scale text lines to fit the whole used area
of the chosen paper size. Try printing to file first to see what the
output looks like.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="font-books">
<title>Font Books</title>

<para>A font book is a printable specimen document showcasing a
collection of fonts. &fontmatrix; makes creation of font books
easy.</para>

<sect1 id="font-books-create">
<title>Creating font books</title>
<para>Suppose you need to create a font book of all fonts your company
designed in 2009.</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>Go to <guilabel>Browse Fonts</guilabel>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>In the field selector choose
<guilabel>Manufacturer</guilabel>, type your company's name and press
<keycap>Enter</keycap>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Enable <guilabel>And</guilabel>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>In the field selector choose
<guilabel>Copyright</guilabel>, type 2009 and press
<keycap>Enter</keycap>.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
<para>Now to actually generate the book:</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>Choose <menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu>
<guimenuitem>Export font book&hellip;</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Pick a template.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Click <guibutton>Browse&hellip;</guibutton> to define
the output PDF file name.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Click <guibutton>OK</guibutton>.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="font-books-templates">
<title>Creating font book templates</title>
<para>Documentation on creating font book templates is not yet
complete. See <xref linkend="resources"/> to ask questions.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="additional">
<title>Additional Tools</title>

<para>&fontmatrix; contains a number of additional tools to help you
be more productive.</para>

<sect1 id="additional-db-check">
<title>Checking the database</title>
<para>Use <menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu>
<guimenuitem>Check Database</guimenuitem></menuchoice> to scan the
&fontmatrix; database for dead links to font files.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="additional-edit-panose">
<title>Editing PANOSE metadata</title>
<para>While PANOSE metadata is helpful for searching, not all fonts
contain it for several reasons:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>not all font types can contain it (PostScript Type 1
fonts have no PANOSE table);</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>not all type designers fill it in;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>some PANOSE metadata is plainly wrong.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>&fontmatrix; provides a way to add or edit PANOSE metadata
non-destructively — by storing changes in its database rather than in
the font files. This also keeps your changes compliant with most
proprietary font licenses.</para>
<para>Pick the font and choose
<menuchoice><guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Edit PANOSE
metadata</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. In the dialog, choose new values
and click <guibutton>OK</guibutton>. From then on, PANOSE searches
will use the corrected data. Currently the only way to reset the
changes is to re-import the font.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="additional-tt-tables">
<title>Viewing TrueType tables</title>
<para>Use <menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu>
<guimenuitem>Show TrueType tables</guimenuitem></menuchoice> to view
the hexadecimal contents of the TrueType tables for the currently
selected font.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="additional-extract">
<title>Extracting fonts from documents</title>
<para>This feature, extracting embedded fonts from documents, relies
on PoDoFo and currently works only for PDF documents.</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>Choose <menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu>
<guimenuitem>Extract fonts&hellip;</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>In <guilabel>Select PDF</guilabel> click
<guibutton>Browse</guibutton> and pick the source PDF.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Enable checkboxes for fonts of interest.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Click <guibutton>Select Dir</guibutton> to choose
where to save extracted fonts.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Click <guibutton>Extract</guibutton>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Click <guibutton>Close</guibutton> when done.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
<para>Notes: fonts are rarely embedded fully — what you typically get
is a subsetted font containing only the glyphs actually used in the
document. &fontmatrix; saves these fonts in PFB format and stores
useful information from the embedded font.</para>
<important>
<para>The &fontmatrix; team strongly discourages users from infringing
copyright. This feature exists for fun and education, not for making
unlicensed profit from someone else's work. If you want to create a
derivative of a font whose license doesn't explicitly allow that,
contact the designer or vendor first.</para>
</important>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="additional-export-info">
<title>Exporting font info for packaging</title>
<para>Use <menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu>
<guimenuitem>Export font info for packaging&hellip;</guimenuitem>
</menuchoice> to fill a template file with metadata for packaging the
currently selected font for a Linux distribution.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="setting-up">
<title>Setting Up</title>

<para>&fontmatrix; is highly configurable. Most options are set once
and forgotten via <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu>
<guimenuitem>Configure &fontmatrix;&hellip;</guimenuitem>
</menuchoice>. Text-layout-engine options are interactive in nature
and live in a separate dialog
(<menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu><guimenuitem>Text layout engine
options</guimenuitem></menuchoice>) which always stays on top.</para>

<sect1 id="prefs-general">
<title>General</title>
<para><guilabel>Ask for tag names when importing fonts</guilabel>
defines whether the import flow opens a tag-assignment dialog. See
<xref linkend="management-importing"/>.</para>
<para><guilabel>Show names of imported fonts after import
process</guilabel> opens a dialog listing the names of the fonts that
were just imported.</para>
<para><guilabel>Show splash at startup</guilabel> hides the splash
screen if disabled. The splash does not delay startup.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="prefs-systray">
<title>System tray</title>
<para><guilabel>Show &fontmatrix; in System Tray</guilabel> enables or
disables system-tray functionality.</para>
<para><guilabel>Close to system tray</guilabel> sends the main window
to the system tray when its close button is clicked. Restore via a
left click on the icon, or right-click → Restore.</para>
<para><guilabel>Start minimized to system tray</guilabel> is useful
when you want &fontmatrix; running in the background but not opening
its window at startup.</para>
<para><guilabel>Show "All" actions</guilabel> adds <emphasis>Activate
all</emphasis> / <emphasis>Deactivate all</emphasis> to the tray
menu.</para>
<para><guilabel>Ask confirmation when activating or deactivating all
fonts</guilabel> and <guilabel>Ask confirmation when activating or
deactivating fonts by tags</guilabel> add safety prompts.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="prefs-display">
<title>Display</title>
<para><guilabel>Preview word</guilabel> controls the preview phrase
displayed in the previews area. Use the placeholders
<literal>&lt;name&gt;</literal>, <literal>&lt;family&gt;</literal>,
<literal>&lt;variant&gt;</literal>.</para>
<para><guilabel>Default font size</guilabel> sets the preview font
size in points.</para>
<para><guilabel>Subtitled</guilabel> toggles a subtitle below each
preview. <guilabel>is RTL</guilabel> renders the preview phrase
right-to-left (e.g. Arabic).</para>
<para><guilabel>Chart Subtitle Font</guilabel> picks the font and size
of the preview subtitles.</para>
<para><guilabel>Information Style</guilabel> lets you use a custom
CSS for the <guilabel>Font Information</guilabel> tab.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="prefs-service">
<title>Service</title>
<para><guilabel>Fonts Editor</guilabel> defines the preferred external
font editor (defaults to FontForge on Linux/macOS).</para>
<para><guilabel>Help Browser</guilabel> selects the browser used for
this help system. (With KHelpCenter integration, this option is no
longer relevant — the system handler is used.)</para>
<para><guilabel>Hyphenation Dictionary</guilabel> picks a dictionary
for sample-text hyphenation.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="prefs-samples">
<title>Samples collection</title>
<para>&fontmatrix; ships with sample texts for many languages. To add
your own, type a name into the field below the list of custom samples
and click <guibutton>+</guibutton>. Then paste your text into the
entry field and click <guibutton>Save</guibutton>. The new sample
appears in the <guilabel>Display options</guilabel> tab.</para>
<para>To remove a custom sample, choose it in the listbox and click
<guibutton>−</guibutton>. This cannot be undone.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="prefs-files">
<title>Files &amp; folders</title>
<para>This tab configures the templates folder, remote font
directories, and local storage paths.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="prefs-shortcuts">
<title>Shortcuts</title>
<para>The Shortcuts tab is now provided by KDE's standard "Configure
Keyboard Shortcuts" dialog
(<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure
Keyboard Shortcuts&hellip;</guimenuitem></menuchoice>). Choose an
action from the list and assign or change its shortcut.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="prefs-layout">
<title>Text layout engine options</title>
<para>&fontmatrix; uses its own text layout engine (TLE) to support
advanced OpenType features. The TLE has a number of variables you can
tune via <menuchoice><guimenu>Service</guimenu><guimenuitem>Text
layout engine options</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para>
<para>The dialog looks like an audio equalizer — drag sliders to
change weights:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Before</guilabel> — factor applied to a
break node before the first OUT_OF_LINE node.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Exact</guilabel> — factor applied to the
first OUT_OF_LINE node.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>After</guilabel> — factor applied to nodes
after the first OUT_OF_LINE node.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>End</guilabel> — same factor for the last
line of the paragraph.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>H-penalty</guilabel> — extra factor applied
when the node is a hyphen.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Max. compression</guilabel> — maximum
compression of white spaces.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="contributing">
<title>Contributing</title>

<para>There are several ways to help make this project better:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>testing and reporting;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>translating user interface and
documentation;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>programming new features and fixing
bugs.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<sect1 id="contributing-testing">
<title>Testing and reporting</title>
<para>Testing is what helps make applications rock-solid. As a
community project, &fontmatrix; relies on its users.</para>

<sect2 id="contributing-crashers">
<title>Crashes</title>
<para>If you find a reproducible crash on Linux, generate a backtrace
with <command>gdb</command>:</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>Install <package>gdb</package>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Open a terminal.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Run <command>gdb fontmatrix</command>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>At the gdb prompt: <command>run</command>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Reproduce the crash.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>At the gdb prompt: <command>bt</command>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Copy the output.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><command>quit</command>.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
<para>On modern KDE Plasma systems, KCrash and DrKonqi capture this
information automatically and offer to file a bug
report.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="contributing-bugreports">
<title>Reporting bugs and requesting features</title>
<para>Submit reports to the <ulink
url="https://github.com/eniac111/fontmatrix/issues">issue
tracker</ulink>. A useful bug report contains: the steps that led to
the bug; the backtrace if it crashed; and information about your
system (distribution, Qt version, &fontmatrix; version).</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="contributing-translating">
<title>Translating</title>
<para>The user interface uses Qt's <literal>.ts</literal> files; help
content is moving to DocBook for translation via
KDE's standard tooling. To start a UI translation:</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>Clone the source: <command>git clone
https://github.com/eniac111/fontmatrix.git</command></para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Configure with
<command>cmake&nbsp;-DTRANSLATOR=&lt;locale&gt;</command>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Run <command>make translation</command>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Open
<filename>fontmatrix-&lt;locale&gt;.ts</filename> in Qt Linguist and
translate.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Run <command>make install</command> to test.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
<para>If your language is right-to-left or uses non-Latin script,
&fontmatrix; should handle that without special configuration.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="contributing-programming">
<title>Programming</title>
<para>The plan is to implement an internal plug-in architecture to
make new features easier to add. If you want to help, see
<xref linkend="resources"/> for contact information.</para>
</sect1>

</chapter>


<chapter id="resources">
<title>Useful Online Resources</title>

<sect1 id="resources-contact">
<title>Contacting developers and fellow users</title>
<para>For questions about using &fontmatrix;, visit the
<ulink url="https://github.com/eniac111/fontmatrix">project
page</ulink>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="resources-fonts">
<title>Looking for free/libre fonts</title>
